Hmm.  OK, I think I see the problem.  You have a particular definition
in your own mind of "gameplay", and where the line is drawn between it
and "interface".  The problem is, that definition isn't the commonly
used one, and as such isn't useful to the discussion, whether you want
it to be or not.

Also, when discussing issues, one uses the general case, not the extreme
corner-case.  The way you play is _extremely_ corner-case from the
perspective of out-of-game note-taking and tracking; while it works for
you, it's not the gameplay experience that most people have, nor what
most people would describe as 'gameplay'.  For that matter, a lot of
people might not even describe it as 'play', but 'work'.

>> That is, how they play it, both strategically and tactically: their
>> gameplay.
> Now you're twisting words to suit you. Gameplay is not the player's  
> individual style. It's how the _game_ plays.

YOU are the one using the non-commonly-accepted definition, Tina.

http://game-research.com/index.php/dictionary/ says:
"Gameplay: Ambiguous term for the total effect of all active game 
elements. Refers to the holistic game experience and the ability 
of the game to command the attention of the player."

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gameplay says:
"Gameplay includes all player experiences during the interaction with
game systems, especially formal games. Proper use is coupled with
reference to 'what the player does'."

_The experience, holistic or otherwise, includes the interface._

Now, in this case, what most players 'does' is _not_ 'write everything down
with a pen and paper'; they just play the game.  Whether it's possible
to write everything down and thus subvert gameplay interaction with mind
flayers and scrolls of amnesia is a moot issue; the general case is that
the vast majority of players don't do this.

Why does this matter?  Because when I, or anyone else, make changes...
especially writing something like a stash-tracker patch, a significant
interface improvement... I need to also be careful of the effects, if
any, it will have on the general case of gameplay.  

In this case, I will be making information readily available to players 
that was not readily available before (key word, 'READILY'; writing it 
all down with pen and paper does NOT qualify), and I need to consider 
whether this is going to make the game easier or potentially (with regard 
to polypiling) make certain strategies far simpler than before.  Someone 
brought up the interaction with mind flayers and whether they should cause 
people to forget their stash location -- THAT is a huge gameplay effect.  

